    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 all tests green (exit 0).\n\n## Summary\n\nI changed telephone handling to store numbers in E.164 form. Three files changed:\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`** \u2014 rewrote `normalizeTelephone`:\n- Strips spaces, dashes and brackets.\n- If a leading `+` is present, keeps it and the country code as-is.\n- Otherwise assumes `+61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits.\n- Requires `+` followed by 8\u201315 digits, else throws `InvalidFieldsException` \u2192 **400**.\n- So `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, `12` \u2192 rejected.\n- Also applied normalization in `updateOwner` (it previously stored the raw value, which would now violate the entity constraint), and updated the duplicate-detection doc \u2014 dedup already compares stored `telephone` values, which are now E.164.\n\n**`Owner.java`** \u2014 replaced the old `@Digits(integer=10)` + `^[0-9]{10}$` pattern with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` (and dropped the now-unused `@Digits` import) so the JPA entity accepts E.164 values.\n\n**`openapi.yml`** \u2014 updated the `telephone` schema description/example to document the E.164 normalization (the input `pattern` already permits `+`).\n\nResults: the 3 acceptance tests (`coreNationalToE164`, `functionalityKeepsExplicitCountryCode`, `errorRejectsUnformattable`) and the full suite pass.",
